<p>China is one of the two anchor markets the Indian shrimp export industry is built around. It took 136,164 MT of Indian frozen shrimp in FY2024-25 — the second-largest single-country market after the United States, and well ahead of the European Union's 99,310 MT, according to MPEDA's own official press release. Across all seafood categories, China was also India's largest export destination by quantity that year: 396,424 MT worth USD 1,276.58 million. This guide is for Chinese seafood importers, trading companies and processors sourcing Indian vannamei: which forms to specify, the GACC/CIFER registration and China-specific residue testing that decide whether a container clears, how the price is built, and what goes wrong on a first order.</p>

<!-- slot:facts -->

<!-- insight:buyer-segmentation -->
<h2>Is China a shrimp market or a wider seafood market for Indian exporters?</h2>
<p>Both, and the split matters when you read the numbers. MPEDA's FY2024-25 release shows China taking 396,424 MT of Indian seafood worth USD 1,276.58 million, but only 136,164 MT of that was frozen shrimp: about 34% of the volume. For the United States the same figures give 311,948 MT of shrimp inside 346,868 MT of seafood, about 90%. China's whole seafood basket averaged about USD 3.22 per kg against about USD 7.83 per kg for the US, because most of what China buys from India is lower-value seafood, not shrimp.</p>
<p>So the China all-seafood average is not a shrimp price, and nor is India's frozen shrimp average across all markets (USD 5,177.01 million for 741,529 MT, about USD 6.98 per kg). Chinese shrimp buyers themselves fall into two groups. Processors and re-packers buy HOSO or HLSO in blocks and add the labour in China. Distributors serving food service and retail in Shanghai and other coastal cities buy PD, PDTO or cooked, often IQF. Scenario: a Qingdao processor and a Shanghai food-service distributor both ask for "vannamei 40/50". One wants block-frozen HOSO for reprocessing; the other wants IQF PD in 1kg retail inners. Their prices per kilo will be far apart, and neither is wrong.</p>
<!-- /insight -->

<h2>Which shrimp forms and counts do Chinese buyers order?</h2>
<p>Specify form (HOSO, HLSO, PD, PUD, PDTO or cooked), count size, and whether you need IQF or block-frozen. Chinese buyers span the full range of use cases — some are importing for further domestic processing (favoring HOSO or HLSO), others for direct retail or food service (often PD or cooked). State the glaze percentage and whether STPP or another phosphate may be used, and at what level, because phosphate-treated shrimp holds more water and that weight is paid for. Tell us your intended use and we'll recommend the right form rather than defaulting to whatever's easiest to quote.</p>

<h2>Which residue limits must a China-bound shipment meet?</h2>
<p>China's own national (GB) standards, not the EU's or the US's. Don't assume another market's compliance transfers — this trips up buyers who source from the same supplier for multiple destinations. China sets its residue and quality limits independently, and product tested and cleared for one market isn't automatically compliant for China. For farmed shrimp the main concern is antibiotic residue, with nitrofurans and chloramphenicol the usual focus. Tell us specifically that China is your destination, and we'll test and document against the applicable Chinese GB standard rather than assuming a US or EU clearance carries over.</p>

<!-- insight:container-economics -->
<h2>How much shrimp fits in a reefer to Shanghai or Qingdao?</h2>
<p>About 12-14 MT in a 20ft reefer and 25-27 MT in a 40ft high-cube reefer, depending on carton configuration and glaze. At the midpoints, 26 MT against 13 MT, the 40ft carries twice as much, so it is the cheaper box per tonne whenever its freight rate to Shanghai, Shenzhen/Yantian or Qingdao is under twice the 20ft rate. A 20ft only makes sense for a trial order or a mixed-count first shipment.</p>
<p>The value inside the box is what makes the paperwork matter. Valued at India's FY2024-25 frozen shrimp average of about USD 6.98 per kg, a 26 MT 40ft carries roughly USD 181,000 of shrimp. That is an average across every form and market, not a China price, but it shows the scale: a container held at the port for a week over a registration mismatch is a large sum held at −18°C, accruing reefer plug-in and demurrage charges.</p>
<!-- /insight -->

<!-- slot:docs -->

<!-- insight:policy-timeline -->
<p><strong>GACC/CIFER registration comes before anything else, and its rules have changed twice in five years.</strong> The timeline a buyer needs to know:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>1 January 2022:</strong> Decree 248 took effect and extended overseas manufacturer registration to all food categories. Food produced on or after that date must carry the registration number (the Chinese registration number or the exporting country's approved number) on both inner and outer packaging.</li>
  <li><strong>Under Decree 248:</strong> renewal had to be applied for 3 to 6 months before the current registration expired. A plant that missed the window could not keep shipping on the old number.</li>
  <li><strong>1 June 2026:</strong> Decree 280 replaced Decree 248. Registrations made under Decree 248 remain valid and CIFER is unchanged; new applications follow Decree 280.</li>
  <li><strong>Throughout:</strong> aquatic products are one of the 17 categories whose manufacturers must first be examined and recommended to GACC by the exporting country's competent authority, so a seafood plant cannot simply self-register.</li>
</ul>
<p>Scenario: a buyer switches to a new Indian packer in mid-2026. The packer's Decree 248 registration is valid, so nothing has to be refiled, but its renewal date falls during the buyer's season. Ask for the registration number and expiry date at the enquiry stage, and confirm the same number is printed on the cartons. Port inspection, the work once done by CIQ before it merged into GACC in 2018, checks both.</p>
<!-- /insight -->
<p>Plan documentation lead time separately from shipping. Transit runs 15-22 days to major Chinese ports (Shanghai, Shenzhen/Yantian, Qingdao) — mid-range compared with our other lanes, shorter than the US route and longer than Southeast Asia — but the GACC/CIFER process is usually the longer pole in the timeline for a first order.</p>
<p>Many Chinese shrimp importers are established seafood trading companies with existing GACC/CIFER registration relationships and customs clearance processes already in place. If you're working with one, the practical hurdles are lower — your main job is agreeing specification, form and pricing, since the regulatory pathway is already proven. If you're a newer entrant or importing for your own processing operation for the first time, budget more time for the registration and clearance learning curve, and lean on us for guidance based on other China-bound shipments we've handled.</p>

<!-- price-behaviour:start -->
<h2>What determines the price of frozen shrimp to China</h2>
<p>Shrimp pricing for China-bound orders moves on the same core variables as any other market, with a couple of China-specific factors layered on top. The main drivers: farm-gate raw material cost in Andhra Pradesh and the other producing states, which is seasonal and tracks pond cycles and disease pressure; count-size availability, since larger counts are structurally scarcer; the form specified (HOSO through cooked all carry different processing yields and labour costs); glaze percentage, which changes the effective price per kilogram even when the quoted number looks identical; freight rates on the China lane, which move with bunker cost and container availability; the rupee-dollar exchange rate at time of shipment; and competitive supply from Ecuador, which competes directly with India for Chinese buyers and can shift demand depending on harvest conditions in a given season. GACC/CIFER-compliant testing to Chinese-specific residue limits, where required for a first shipment on a new registration, can also add a modest cost and lead time that should be planned for rather than treated as a surprise.</p>
<p>We quote FOB or CIF, on net weight with glaze stated separately, and confirm pricing at the point of order rather than holding a fixed rate card. To get an accurate quote, tell us: product form, count size, glaze percentage, order quantity, destination port, target shipment month, and your GACC/CIFER registration status.</p>
