<!-- insight:documentation-failures -->
<h2>What actually goes wrong on a China-bound shrimp shipment</h2>
<p>The single most common failure point on this lane isn't product quality in the usual sense — it's a shipment arriving at a Chinese port without the receiving facility's GACC/CIFER registration properly in place, or with a mismatch between the registered facility details and what's declared on the shipping documents. Chinese customs checks the registration record against the declaration, and any inconsistency (a facility name, address or product-category mismatch) can hold a container at port while it's resolved — costly in demurrage and, for a first-time buyer, alarming if they weren't expecting it. We treat confirming registration status as a mandatory first question on any new China enquiry precisely because of how often this trips up buyers who assumed a general seafood-import relationship covered it automatically.</p>
<p>The second recurring issue is residue-limit mismatches: a lot expecting to be tested against China's specific maximum residue limits, but was tested (or only tested) against EU or US limits because that's what the exporter's default protocol covers. China's limits aren't identical to either, so a "clean" test result from another market's protocol doesn't guarantee compliance here — we test specifically to Chinese MRLs once we know China is the destination, which is exactly why telling us the destination market early matters more on this route than on most others.</p>
<p>On the physical side, count-size and grading disputes follow the same pattern as other markets — tolerance bands should be agreed and stated on the proforma invoice before loading, not discovered at receiving inspection. Fumigation of wooden pallets needs the same ISPM-15 lead time as any other lane, and stuffing a reefer container correctly for the 15-22 day transit matters less here than on the longer US route, but pre-cooling the container before loading still meaningfully protects product quality on arrival. The most common mistake we see from first-time China buyers is treating GACC/CIFER registration as the exporter's problem alone and not asking about it until an order is already placed — it's genuinely a shared responsibility, and starting the conversation early is the single biggest thing a new buyer can do to avoid delay.</p>
<p>Scenario: a 40ft of PD 31/40 reaches Qingdao. The health certificate and the CIFER record name the plant correctly, but the outer cartons were printed from an older artwork file without the registration number. Customs holds the container while the importer argues the case, and the shrimp is a labelling problem, not a quality one. Send us your carton artwork for checking against the registration record before production starts.</p>
<!-- /insight -->
